  {
    "id": 2095,
    "date": "2023-09-11T18:45:17+00:00",
    "from": "tour2020",
    "text": "The end of the Santa Fe Trail is in Santa Fe, New Mexico.",
    "media": "2095.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": 2097,
    "date": "2023-09-11T18:57:14+00:00",
    "from": "tour2020",
    "text": "El Monte, California is not actually the end of the Santa Fe Trail. The Santa Fe Trail ended in Santa Fe, New Mexico.\n\nThere are a few reasons why El Monte is sometimes referred to as the \"End of the Santa Fe Trail.\"\n\n - **The Southern Branch of the Santa Fe Trail.** In the 1840s, a branch of the Santa Fe Trail was established that connected Santa Fe to Los Angeles via El Monte. This branch of the trail was called the Southern Branch of the Santa Fe Trail, or the Old Spanish Trail.\n\n - **The Gold Rush.** In 1848, the California Gold Rush began, and many people traveled from the eastern United States to California via the Santa Fe Trail. Many of these travelers stopped in El Monte to rest and resupply before continuing on to California.\n\n - **The Promotion of El Monte.** In the early 20th century, El Monte officials began to promote the city as the \"End of the Santa Fe Trail.\" They did this in order to attract tourists and businesses to the city.\n\nWhile El Monte is not technically the end of the Santa Fe Trail, it did play an important role in the history of the trail. The city is a reminder of the many people who traveled the Santa Fe Trail in search of new opportunities and a better life.\n\nThe claim that El Monte is the end of the Santa Fe Trail is disputed by some, including the Santa Fe Trail Historical Society in New Mexico.\n\n---",
    "media": null
  },
  {
    "id": 2098,
    "date": "2023-09-11T19:15:35+00:00",
